{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact R13 item 4 head cfa0f2122629ec1a0ae4e67eeb8c24abbbf8bcbf on builder/read-token-quota-r13-20261109, tree f646cfd0, all 36 artifacts, for request 90878601 under promise 3a1d2b78 and review request 02ab7b7d. A Destination now admits at most eight open read-token reservations per authenticated member, and the native commit refuses a ninth with `limit` before it writes any entry.\n\nWhat I checked in the source:\n\n- The bucket. `mintOf` keys the bucket on `canonicalize(input.authority[0].subject)`. `Grant.subject` is the judged grant's `MemberRef`, so it holds the Membership ScopeRef and the member. The key and the principal do not choose the bucket. `read-token` always carries exactly one grant, `destination.read-token`, so the `authority.length !== 1` throw is unreachable for an admitted act. Rows carry the full Destination ScopeRef, so incarnations never share a bucket.\n- Admission. `check` runs in the judge, after `admissionRefusal` and before `verdict: \"write\"`. It reads at most eight unexpired bucket rows on the commit's one clock reading. `sealed` runs in the entry's transaction callback, before `told` and `rows.sealed`. It deletes that bucket's expired rows and inserts the new reservation at entry time plus requested hours plus ten minutes. Because each insert first deletes the bucket's expired rows, a bucket never holds more than eight expired rows. The count ignores expired rows, so admission does not depend on the alarm.\n- Retries. An accepted-key retry returns from `accepted-before` and never reaches `check` or `sealed`.\n- The adapter. `bind` sets the handle before the provider await, only on an open row whose handle is still unset. P16 has one attempt. An interrupted call can therefore never mint again: a second `bind` finds the handle set and returns null, and the operation stays unknown. `minted:false` deletes only the row with the bound handle. A known reply moves `ends_ms` to the provider's end. A reply after the reservation expired fails `minted`, so `putReadReply` stores `{token, ends}` with null plaintext and reopens no slot. That matches ruling 6cf4c1c8. Legacy operations, those before the scope's first reservation sequence, keep the original path and count in no bucket.\n- Expiry. `CredentialStore.expire` runs the quota's 64-row quantum in its `finally`, so the quantum still runs when the backfill throws. `expiryWake` takes the earlier of the custody and quota ends. A quota end past `LAST_MS` schedules no alarm, and a quiet store still reads no clock.\n- The public reason. `limit` enters `RefusalReason`, the bytes decoder, both exhaustive metric maps and the MCP refusal table. The client witness accepts `limit` only with `judgedAt` and still rejects an unknown reason.\n\nWhat holds:\n\n- Scope. Main f9eb50bc is an ancestor, the head is pushed, and the builder's worktree is clean at the head. Against main, the head changes exactly the 36 paths that its 36 artifacts cover. The commit-message check passes: 22 checked, 0 flagged. No dependency, lockfile, Wrangler, definition, genesis or migration file changes.\n- The seals. I recomputed the four whole-file seals outside the builder's worktree. read-token-quota.ts gives 73a3df3e…, credential-store.ts e1dacade…, destination-host.ts c09958d9… and records.ts 05bf74f7…, each equal to its literal. The named-owner seals for core.ts `Scope` and object.ts `ScopeObject` pass in the gate's Node stage. Each sealed change is the one described above. No route check, authority check or check order moves.\n- The gate. I read gate.exit at e180420c: \"exit 0\". All five stages passed: kernel, Node, Counting, Plain and Native. before.json and after.json are byte-equal. After the gate, only the note and two manifests change. GitHub reports Page run 42602786995 and Card run 42602787058 as successful at e180420c.\n- The control. At 00239447, removing only Core's `limit` return fails both Native cases at their ninth-limit assertions, lines 136 and 239. The owner exits 1, the verifier exits 0, there are no unhandled errors, and the source is restored. core.ts and read-token-quota.test.ts at the head equal the control's recorded originals, 52f71cd7… and e2cd6d8e….\n- The tests. The Native owner uses real membership authority, transactions, restart, alarms and the Artifacts adapter, with scripted provider replies. It covers several keys of one member, another member, retries, rollback, explicit refusal, the unknown hold, and the late reply after a replacement takes the eighth place. The SQLite owner covers legacy rows, incarnations, the 64-row quantum and the last representable clock. No case, assertion or deadline is removed.\n- The evidence. The gate, browser, control and focused-closure manifests hash to the values that the note cites. Their 61 files match their sizes and digests and are read-only, and the credential scan finds no match. The note keeps all eight failed focused units failed.\n\nArchitecture: sound. The quota is runtime service code behind one optional port. Its SQL tables are private, its reservation shares the entry's transaction, and the adapter owns the provider-side transitions. The public contract gains one reason, under kernel section 7.1's exception for filed security fixes.\n\nSecurity: no finding. A member cannot change their bucket by switching keys. A refused ninth writes nothing. A lost reply cannot reopen its slot or mint twice, and a late reply discloses no plaintext. One limit holds by design, and the note states it: an unknown reservation can expire while the provider token still lives, so the quota bounds reservations, not live provider tokens.\n\nSimplification: no required change. One note: in `minted`, the local `changed` adds nothing over returning the comparison directly.\n\nThe planner deploys the runtime and the Page bundle after landing. The two private tables start empty. This approval authorizes no deployment and closes no live acceptance.","body":{"artifact"